WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:05.520
With seven main saga installments, an anthology
movie, and numerous television releases and

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:10.389
animated spinoffs, Star Wars is undoubtedly
one of the biggest Hollywood properties ever.

00:00:10.389 --> 00:00:14.990
However, just being a Star Wars movie isn't
enough to guarantee success, and there have

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:17.279
definitely been some missteps along the way.

00:00:17.279 --> 00:00:21.380
We're look at you, Lumpy.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:24.720
Here's a look back at all the movies that
are part of the massive phenomenon that is

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.560
Star Wars, as we rank them in order from worst
to best.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.890
Star Wars: The Clone Wars

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:35.950
While 2008's Star Wars: The Clone Wars made
history as the first fully animated Star Wars

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:38.210
feature-length film, it isn't great.

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:42.960
Essentially a TV pilot packaged for the big
screens, it was almost universally panned

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.650
by critics and audiences.

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:49.480
With a script filled with stilted dialogue
and forced tension, The Clone Wars just wasn't

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:51.000
suited for the big screen.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.710
Nevertheless, it succeeded at the box office,
and worked as a vehicle to introduce audiences

00:00:55.710 --> 00:01:00.940
to Cartoon Network's The Clone Wars animated
series—which went on to be extremely popular.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:02.390
Star Wars Holiday Special

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:09.680
Until The Clone Wars came along, 1978's infamous
Star Wars Holiday Special reigned as the absolute

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.710
worst Star Wars movie.

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:16.790
The show starts with ten minutes of untranslated
Wookiee dialogue with no Han Solo around to

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:20.810
translate it, and things go bizarrely downhill
from there.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:26.250
Segments include a Bea Arthur musical number,
humorless comedy bits, cameos by Hollywood

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:31.720
stars, and an appearance by the rock band
Jefferson Starship, because, well, their name

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:33.390
has the word 'Starship' in it.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:37.530
The one bright spot of the Holiday Special
is a cartoon introducing the character of

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:41.050
Boba Fett, but the rest is like watching a
slow-motion trainwreck.

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:43.590
For his part, George Lucas later claimed:

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:49.090
"If I had the time and a sledgehammer, I would
track down every copy of that show and smash

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:50.090
it."

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:52.980
At least we know what he'll be doing during
his retirement.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:53.980
The Ewok Adventure

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:59.580
While the Star Wars Holiday Special has a
mostly nonsensical plot, the simplistic sci-fi

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:04.710
fairytale in 1984’s The Ewok Adventure at
least has a beginning, middle, and end.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:09.030
This key difference—as well as the massively
better production values—push it past the

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:11.620
much-maligned 1978 special.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:17.489
Still, after it aired on November 25, 1984,
the reviews were lukewarm at best—which

00:02:17.489 --> 00:02:19.980
isn't surprising given the young target audience.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.510
But they weren't done with Ewoks just yet.

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:24.779
Ewoks: The Battle for Endor

00:02:24.779 --> 00:02:29.129
The Ewok Adventure was the first of two spinoff
films set on Endor.

00:02:29.129 --> 00:02:35.049
The 1985 sequel Ewoks: The Battle for Endor
continues the tale begun in the first TV movie,

00:02:35.049 --> 00:02:37.730
but with a slightly meatier and more serious
plot.

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:42.219
Like its predecessor, The Battle for Endor
won an Emmy, had a brief overseas theatrical

00:02:42.219 --> 00:02:45.370
run, and made it onto home media in various
forms.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:49.260
The Battle for Endor is marginally better
than The Ewok Adventure—but even with its

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:54.090
stronger plot, this sequel isn't deep enough
to make a lasting impression… even with

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:55.969
an ornery Wilford Brimley.

00:02:55.969 --> 00:02:58.819
Episode I – The Phantom Menace

00:02:58.819 --> 00:03:04.860
In 1999, the hype surrounding the new Star
Wars prequel trilogy was off the charts.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:09.959
Unfortunately, Episode I: The Phantom Menace
was a complete letdown—even for many the

00:03:09.959 --> 00:03:12.120
young fans it was geared towards.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:17.769
Did George Lucas really think kids would want
to watch a movie about embargoes, taxation,

00:03:17.769 --> 00:03:19.370
and political intrigue?

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:23.780
While visually impressive, The Phantom Menace
gets lost in its own narrative...and that's

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.540
even before Jar Jar Binks shows up.

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:29.459
"Don't do that again."

00:03:29.459 --> 00:03:33.890
There are a few saving graces in The Phantom
Menace—namely, Ian McDiarmid's performance

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:39.390
as Palpatine, just about everything with Darth
Maul, and the battle on Naboo are bright spots

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:41.510
in an otherwise pretty dull movie.

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:45.689
It's not the bottom of the barrel, but The
Phantom Menace is clearly the worst of the

00:03:45.689 --> 00:03:48.499
main Skywalker saga films—so far.

00:03:48.499 --> 00:03:50.959
Episode II – Attack of the Clones

00:03:50.959 --> 00:03:55.809
Following the The Phantom Menace, fans crossed
their fingers that Lucas could manage to pull

00:03:55.809 --> 00:03:57.959
things together for the next installment.

00:03:57.959 --> 00:04:01.730
If there's one thing we can say for Attack
of the Clones, it's that it's better than

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:03.129
The Phantom Menace.

00:04:03.129 --> 00:04:07.590
It has some of the more entertaining action
sequences seen thus far in the entire saga.

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:12.919
The fight between Obi-Wan and Jango Fett on
Kamino and the later battle scene on Geonosis

00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:15.279
are wonderful examples of action done right.

00:04:15.279 --> 00:04:19.449
But even with all those positives, what really
hurts this movie more than anything is the

00:04:19.449 --> 00:04:20.930
awful dialogue.

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:25.440
You could even call it "unbelievably" bad,
but only if you haven't seen The Phantom Menace.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:30.530
Also, this scene of Anakin riding a cow-shaped
watermelon didn't help one bit.

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:33.150
Episode III – Revenge of the Sith

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:37.449
A modern rewatch of Revenge of the Sith allows
its good points to shine through, without

00:04:37.449 --> 00:04:41.460
being clouded by the hate that was associated
with the prequels back during its initial

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:42.460
release.

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:47.001
To start with, the film is much darker than
most of the other Star Wars films, being the

00:04:47.001 --> 00:04:51.010
first in the saga to garner a well-deserved
PG-13 rating.

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:55.460
Many of these dark moments — like Order
66 and the Jedi Purge — are surprisingly

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:56.460
brutal.

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:01.630
Additionally, the lightsaber battles in Revenge
of the Sith are more exciting and better-choreographed

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:03.090
than any that came before.

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:07.819
Paired with John Williams' amazing score,
the battles between Obi-Wan and Anakin and

00:05:07.819 --> 00:05:12.319
between Yoda and Sidious are some of the most
impressive in the entire franchise.

00:05:12.319 --> 00:05:16.970
As the third film in a trilogy, Sith also
ties up Anakin's arc with his transformation

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:17.970
to Vader.

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:22.490
Yes, it suffers from the same rough dialogue
and overacting as the rest of the prequels,

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:26.689
but Revenge of The Sith stands solidly as
the best of that series.

00:05:26.689 --> 00:05:28.870
Episode VI – Return of the Jedi

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:34.810
Yes, Return of the Jedi is a classic, and
fans who grew up with the original films don't

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:36.629
like to admit many of its faults.

00:05:36.629 --> 00:05:39.229
But that doesn't mean those faults don't exist.

00:05:39.229 --> 00:05:45.159
The most glaring of those has to be the Endor
section, at least where the Ewoks are involved.

00:05:45.159 --> 00:05:48.790
And while Han's rescue at the beginning of
the film is very enjoyable — especially

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:53.160
the comic relief provided by Han's blindness
— the Tatooine sequence tends to drag on

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:54.490
a little too long.

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:58.840
Although many fans felt a little cheated by
the rehashing of the Death Star plot, the

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:02.561
space battle around the Death Star turned
out to be one of the best in the history of

00:06:02.561 --> 00:06:03.860
the franchise.

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:08.990
And you can't argue too hard against the story's
closure, especially watching Vader come full-circle

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:10.789
and regain his humanity.

00:06:10.789 --> 00:06:15.419
At the end of the day, Return of the Jedi
is a solid, enjoyable, and flawed part of

00:06:15.419 --> 00:06:19.060
the Star Wars saga—no matter which version
you're watching.

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:21.550
Rogue One: A Star Wars Story

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:24.520
Rogue One stands mostly on its own merits.

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:29.210
It doesn't rely on too many familiar characters
and allows Disney and Lucasfilm to explore

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:34.080
new stories in the galaxy that don't revolve
around the Skywalker family and their pals.

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:38.699
At its heart, Rogue One is a war movie, not
a typical Star Wars space opera.

00:06:38.699 --> 00:06:42.870
It's filled with great action sequences and
a cast of supporting characters that you actually

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:44.290
start to care about.

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:49.289
Additionally, it gives us yet another believable
and strong female hero in Jyn Erso

00:06:49.289 --> 00:06:54.509
On the negative side, some of the dialogue
is clunky, and the CGI used to recreate Grand

00:06:54.509 --> 00:06:58.860
Moff Tarkin and Princess Leia ventures into
Uncanny Valley more than once.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:04.280
That said, these are mostly minor issues with
what is overall a very solid Star Wars film.

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:07.030
Episode VII – The Force Awakens

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:11.120
When Lucasfilm and Disney first announced
plans for a new Star Wars trilogy, fans were

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:14.000
apprehensive that we'd get another run of
prequels.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:18.680
Thankfully, the producers and director J.J.
Abrams pulled out all the stops, resulting

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:21.400
in one of the best movies in the entire saga.

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:24.680
There's no question that The Force Awakens
justifies the hype.

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:27.159
It appeals to fans old and new alike.

00:07:27.159 --> 00:07:30.270
The new characters are all excellent additions
to the franchise.

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:34.669
The showdown between Han and Kylo and the
final lightsaber battle in the woods are two

00:07:34.669 --> 00:07:36.449
major standout scenes.

00:07:36.449 --> 00:07:41.470
The major downside is that much the characters
and plot of The Force Awakens parallel A New

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:46.599
Hope, especially Rey's arc and the Death Star
stand-in, Starkiller Base.

00:07:46.599 --> 00:07:51.150
That aside, it's still an excellent entry
in the Star Wars series, with a familiar yet

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:55.060
fresh story and a new cast of faces ready
to take the saga forward.

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:58.180
Episode VIII - The Last Jedi

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:02.770
Director Rian Johnson definitely didn't play
it safe with his first crack at the Star Wars

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:03.770
saga.

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:07.560
Instead, he delivered a visually stunning
and narratively satisfying film that echoes

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:11.960
the darkness of The Empire Strikes Back without
being chained to its legacy.

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:17.550
Although The Last Jedi has earned almost universal
critical acclaim, it isn't a perfect movie.

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:21.169
Some of the subplots could have done with
some trimming down, and several of the more

00:08:21.169 --> 00:08:23.349
comedic moments were jarring.

00:08:23.349 --> 00:08:28.199
That said, the movie also provides excellent
character development and absolutely jaw-dropping

00:08:28.199 --> 00:08:31.409
action sequences, which help make up for its
shortcomings.

00:08:31.409 --> 00:08:34.500
Episode V – The Empire Strikes Back

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:39.900
After the amazing success of A New Hope, George
Lucas had a near-impossible task ahead of

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:43.510
him when it came to making a sequel to such
a worldwide sensation.

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:47.840
Fortunately, The Empire Strikes Back was a
resounding success.

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:51.890
Darker and more intense than the original,
The Empire Strikes Back suffers only for the

00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:54.840
lack of a clear beginning or ending to the
story.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:58.040
And even in that regard, it's hard to fault
the film.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:03.060
The second story in a trilogy is always the
hardest to get right, and for many fans, the

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:05.140
second installment was the best.

00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:10.290
Regardless, The Empire Strikes Back is an
indispensable film, both within the Star Wars

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:13.580
universe and for the science fiction genre
as a whole.

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:15.810
Episode IV – A New Hope

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:20.520
With so many things in life, you can't top
the original — and that applies even to

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:21.720
Star Wars.

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:26.410
Ever since its theatrical release four decades
ago, A New Hope has reigned supreme as the

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:28.220
best Star Wars movie.

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:33.400
This isn't just nostalgia talking: A New Hope
broke ground in so many ways and still stands

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:35.760
up to a critical viewing today.

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:40.210
While at its heart A New Hope is a traditional
underdog tale of good versus evil, the way

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:45.510
that George Lucas tells this story in the
framework of a galaxy far, far away made cinematic

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:46.510
history.

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:50.430
Never before had a science-fiction film been
approached with the grimy and aged look that

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:55.700
Star Wars had, and Lucas' team brought new
model-making and photographic advances to

00:09:55.700 --> 00:09:57.830
the movie industry with their work on A New
Hope.

00:09:57.830 --> 00:10:02.831
Beyond the historic significance on the film,
the original Star Wars is a great movie in

00:10:02.831 --> 00:10:05.510
terms of story, pacing, and action.

00:10:05.510 --> 00:10:10.020
Not only is A New Hope the best movie in the
Star Wars franchise, it still stands as one

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:11.750
of the best films of all time.

00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:13.160
Thanks for watching!

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:15.680
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:16.932
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

